and doing operations
all the way.

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:37.830
We have been introducing
Microsoft technology

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:41.550
that is the native stack,
like Event Grid, Event Hubs,

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:43.200
and also Azure Data Explorer,

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.930
that changed dramatically
the way it has been working.

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:51.480
Our data scientists now have
time to really go for patterns

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:57.240
that are telling us what is
actually wrong with the system,

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:00.450
and at the end, which are
helping our customers

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:02.970
and which are helping
the patient to make sure

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:05.100
that a system is
running smoothly.

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:06.270
YITZHAK KESSELMAN: So
thank you, Dr. Werner.

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.070
So you moved from a
kind of something

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.920
that was a bit more bad,
something a bit more streaming

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:14.460
into the cloud using Azure Plus
services, but you also move now

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:15.900
to Fabric real-time
intelligence.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:18.990
So tell us a bit more how
it changed kind of --

00:36:18.990 --> 00:36:18.991
[inaudible]

00:36:18.991 --> 00:36:20.400
-- the experience
for your developers

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:23.520
and what was the impact
on your end customers.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:24.870
DR. WERNER ZIRKEL: I
think for developers,

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:27.210
it's interesting in two ways.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:30.180
So Fabric is like a
natural success of --

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:31.170
for which we have been --

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:36.270
the technology we have been
using before, but it has two --

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:37.710
impact on two aspects.

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:39.480
The first one is development.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:43.080
So development speed
increases really much

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:46.350
because all the things
that you need to --

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:50.490
nitty-gritty details that will
be taken over and automated.

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:51.240
That's number one.

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:53.640
Second one is operations.

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.760
We don't have the time
to operate like VMs,

00:36:56.760 --> 00:37:00.840
cluster service, what have you,
so that has been taken over.

00:37:00.840 --> 00:37:03.960
It's all automated so we
have really decreased

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:06.960
in operational costs at the end.

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:08.550
YITZHAK KESSELMAN: Now, so
that's exciting to hear.

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:10.890
That's exactly the goal, to
make sure that developers focus

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:13.110
on the specific IP,
their knowledge,

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:15.720
and they are not focusing
on the plumbing or kind

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:16.770
of maintaining the system.

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:19.470
So happy to hear that it worked
for you and your customers.

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:22.290
So what was kind of impact
on all up on your business,

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:23.730
if you can share
on your customers

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:27.360
that are now using
Siemens Healthineers?

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:30.120
DR. WERNER ZIRKEL: I think we
are coming now into this mode

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:34.110
where streaming is
really like a commodity.

00:37:34.110 --> 00:37:38.070
So our systems need to be able
to stream data in real time,

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:42.390
so and this is a big increase
that we can go with Fabric,

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:45.120
because it helps
really to make sure

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:46.920
that reactive service finds --

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:50.640
hits the point exactly where we
have a problem in the system,

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:53.070
we can order spare
parts, if required,

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:57.060
and prevent downtimes
for the customer.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.580
So I think this
will increase also

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.300
from a customer
side, the benefit.

00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:04.560
YITZHAK KESSELMAN: Oh, thank
you so much, Dr. Werner.

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:05.790
It was a pleasure having you.

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:07.200
Thank you for joining me today.

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:08.910
DR. WERNER ZIRKEL:
You're welcome.

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:10.260
YITZHAK KESSELMAN: So
that was super exciting

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:11.490
to hear Dr. Werner share

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.560
about how the product team
helps their customers,

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:16.530
but more how their developers
are becoming more productive

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:18.270
and really able to
focus on therapy

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:21.450
and their specific skills,
and not on the plumbing.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:22.650
So with that, let's focus

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:24.450
on the next thing what
we're going to do.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:25.200
In this case, we're going

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:27.900
to discuss how really Fabric
IQ can enable both people

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:30.570
and agents to work with
all this data we created.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:32.550
And today everyone
talks about AI,

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:34.230
but the question
how we can apply it

00:38:34.230 --> 00:38:36.600
to really generate real
value in this case.

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.610
And speaking about AI,
there are two aspects here,

00:38:38.610 --> 00:38:41.250
one is the machine learning
and the other one is LLMs,

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:42.840
large language models.

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:45.780
And the goal here is really
to use where AI excels,

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:48.540
really finding the needle in the
haystack and ability to reason

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:51.420
about it to really understand
how we can use those signals

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:54.450
to help drive our operations
for our business.

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:56.790
And that's where Fabric IQ
creates the difference.

00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:59.220
Today, as I mentioned before,
we are working on tables

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:01.110
and schemas on the raw data,

00:39:01.110 --> 00:39:02.670
without the context
of the business.

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.490
And the goal here is really to
power the agents to be grounded

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:07.740
on the real business
context, the real ability

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:10.650
of what the business represents,
all the actions, all the rules,

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:13.230
all the policies, that we really
represent the virtual brain

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:14.400
of the business.

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:17.640
And if you look kind of at the
spectrum of agents from no-code

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:20.700
to pro-code where developers
build their own custom agents,

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:23.160
as you are, you want to
make sure that those agents

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:26.460
that you build are grounded
on the ontology in Fabric IQ

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:29.490
and powered by real-time
intelligence, and to dive deep

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:31.860
into kind of what
Microsoft tells us to do.

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:33.930
So all the integration
from Microsoft Foundry,

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:36.360
Microsoft Copilot Studio,
and out-of-the-box agent

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.000
from Microsoft Fabric are
all grounded and integrated

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:41.730
on the ontologies in Fabric IQ.

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:43.710
So with that, I would like to
invite Tessa to show you more

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:48.450
in depth how we -- you can
use AI with ontologies.

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:48.990
TESSA KLOSTER: Thank you.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:53.220
Okay, yes, let's go right in
to show a demo about agents,

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:56.070
specifically the Operations
Agent capabilities,

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:59.280
which as I mentioned before is
actually generally available.

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:01.770
So let's see how it works.

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.200
So going right in, we're
actually going to start --

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:05.850
if you remember before
from the demos,

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:09.210
we showed how you can start
to automate based off

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:12.360
of the custom data as it's
streaming in or maybe

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:14.640
as you omit those
business events.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:16.350
But what I'm going to do
here is actually start

00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:18.600
by creating an anomaly detector.

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:22.290
And what this is going to do
is leverage the same data

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:25.860
that I have in Eventhouse, but
it's going to bring in the power

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:30.930
of data science, it's going to
analyze and understand the data,

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:33.270
the different patterns that
are existing in my data,

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:35.190
and it's going to
recommend the best model

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:38.010
to detect anomalies on my data.

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:39.540
And so I'm not a data scientist,

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:42.990
but you can see here it
leverages user-friendly

00:40:42.990 --> 00:40:45.870
terminology, things like
"fast outlier scanner,"

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:47.580
"core pattern finder."

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:50.100
And because I can see my
data right alongside this

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:53.580
information, I can better
understand what this model is

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:57.030
going to do and if it matches
the anomalies that I understand

00:40:57.030 --> 00:40:58.890
within my business data.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:02.310
So very powerful, in just a few
clicks, I can actually create

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:05.280
and publish this anomaly
detector, and what it's going

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:07.950
to do is as the data's streaming
in, as new media comes in,

00:41:07.950 --> 00:41:09.870
it's going to run
that same analysis

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:13.050
and actually emit an
anomaly detector event

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:16.860
when something meets
those conditions.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:17.790
And so very powerful.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:20.040
Again, you saw it before
when I did this manually,

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:22.920
now we're fully automating
it by also leveraging AI

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:26.130
to actually detect the anomalies
themselves instead of me having

00:41:26.130 --> 00:41:29.910
to write custom code in
order to understand them.

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:32.220
I can even -- when I subscribe
to the anomaly detector,

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:35.640
I can filter to specific
values, and just like that,

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:38.670
once the alert's created, it
can run alongside my team,

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:40.920
and alert, and then
notify the team.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:42.660
But we're going to take it
even further, actually,

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:44.430
with the Operations Agent.

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:46.700
And what this is actually going
to do is it's going to be able

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:49.310
to monitor and understand
all of this different data,

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:53.330
the anomaly detector events, my
event host data, my ontology.

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:56.690
It's going to do all of this in
real time and actually be able

00:41:56.690 --> 00:42:00.050
to monitor, and understand,
and then most importantly,

00:42:00.050 --> 00:42:02.630
act alongside my business.

00:42:02.630 --> 00:42:03.890
So I'm going to start

00:42:03.890 --> 00:42:06.770
by onboarding the agent is
I would any team member

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:07.970
and give it the goals.

00:42:07.970 --> 00:42:10.970
In this case, it's going to help
ensure that our stock levels

00:42:10.970 --> 00:42:14.030
for the concession
stands are adequate.

00:42:14.030 --> 00:42:15.900
I can provide them more
detailed instructions,

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:17.280
as I would for any agent.

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:19.590
But the real power comes in
when I connect it directly

00:42:19.590 --> 00:42:21.000
to that same ontology.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:25.020
So all of the business context,
the entities, the relationships,

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:26.340
the actions that are already

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:29.130
in the ontology are
automatically available

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:30.240
to this agent.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.540
I can add custom actions as
well, and just like that,

00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:34.980
I've provided all
this information

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:37.380
on the left-hand side, the
goals, the instructions,

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:40.470
the knowledge from the ontology,
and the custom actions

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:42.510
that we want for this
particular case.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:45.210
And just like that, I can
generate the playbook.

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:47.010
Now, when I select "Generate the
Playbook," what you're going

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:50.750
to see on the right-hand side is
essentially the agent telling

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:54.230
back to us what it understood
about all of this information.

00:42:54.230 --> 00:42:56.210
So you can see here it found
the different entities

00:42:56.210 --> 00:42:58.820
that are relevant for the
goals that we provided,

00:42:58.820 --> 00:43:02.150
it sees the properties that
are interesting and the rules

00:43:02.150 --> 00:43:03.860
that it's not just
going to monitor for,

00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:07.100
but actually take actions on
when the conditions are met.

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:09.620
So now that the Operations
Agent is up and running,

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:12.300
it's alongside the rest of
our team, actually directly

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:14.520
in our team's channel,
and you can see, right,

00:43:14.520 --> 00:43:17.190
that it already found
a particular condition

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:19.050
that it's alerting on.

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:22.320
So in this case, it can see
a specific concession has

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:23.280
low stock.

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:25.110
It's recommending an
action to reorder,

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:27.960
but I can also take the option
to investigate further.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:29.550
And so in this case, the
agent is going to go

00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:31.920
that deeper level, do
some root cause analysis

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:37.380
to really understand what led to
this condition to really ensure

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:40.260
that the action it's
suggesting is the best one.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:41.760
In this case, it's
still the right action

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.350
to reorder the stock,
it's going to help --

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:46.980
ask me to confirm the different
parameters, and just like that,

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:50.250
we automatically
not just identified

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:52.500
and understood a
particular issue,

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.690
actually took action
to resolve it.

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:56.430
And so whenever we have
these agents running,

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:59.310
it's very important to
understand what's going on.

00:43:59.310 --> 00:44:03.030
So we have the out-of-box
activity log capabilities

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:05.250
that are going to show
not just what happened,

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:08.880
it's going to see the -- what
was run, what was executed,

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:11.400
what it did about it, the
alert or the action it ended

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.370
up taking, to really help me
build confidence in the agent,

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:15.570
but also refine it further

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:19.080
so that it's working the
best alongside my business.

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:21.630
So that really showed some of
the out-of-box capabilities

00:44:21.630 --> 00:44:23.970
that we have for
Operations Agent.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:25.710
Really quickly, we have
other capabilities

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:26.910
across the entire system.

00:44:26.910 --> 00:44:30.720
You can build a RAG solution
directly with Eventhouse.

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:32.160
I think I already mentioned,

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:34.380
we have the different anomaly
detector capabilities.

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:36.810
So this is in public preview
right now, very easy for you

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:39.750
to go and connect to your
existing Eventhouse data.

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:44.580
Data agent is generally
available in Fabric IQ as well,

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:48.300
so you can use this to use -- to
ask natural language questions,

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:49.050
and then, of course,

00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:52.320
the Operations Agent generally
available in Fabric IQ.

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:54.870
So all of these different
capabilities out of box

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:59.160
to make it very easy for you to
leverage your data in Fabric.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:03.450
You can also use Foundry
to directly connect

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:07.140
to that same ontology, the
same information across all

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:09.720
of your Fabric data estate

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:12.540
to build those custom
agents as well.

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:15.630
So with that, I'll hand
it back to Yitzhak.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:16.860
YITZHAK KESSELMAN:
Thank you, Tessa.

00:45:16.860 --> 00:45:18.480
And I'm super excited
also to share

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:19.830
with you how you can
take it further,

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:22.170
how you can use
all those signals

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:24.330
from the real world
together with the ontology

00:45:24.330 --> 00:45:27.060
to present the brain of the
business, but also move it

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:27.960
to the physical world.

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:30.450
And that's where physical
comes into place.

00:45:30.450 --> 00:45:32.850
And I'm excited to share about
our strategic partnership

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:35.670
with NVIDIA, where we combined
all those capabilities together

00:45:35.670 --> 00:45:38.790
to really help to drive
and power physical AI.

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:41.910
So if we look at the examples
of airport operations,

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:43.830
in this scenario, there
are multiple entities,

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:45.720
there are robotic arms,
there are conveyor belts,

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:47.370
baggage, cameras, et cetera.

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:50.070
And the question how we can
collect all those signals,

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:53.880
analyze them, understand and
act to them, both with humans

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:56.100
and agents, but also
in the physical world,

00:45:56.100 --> 00:45:57.990
how we can use Fabric
real-time intelligence

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:00.180
to collect all those
signals that are coming

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:03.840
in real time using Fabric IQ,
using the ontology studio

00:46:03.840 --> 00:46:05.970
to present the contextualized
the visual grain

00:46:05.970 --> 00:46:08.280
of these operations,
using NVIDIA Omniverse

00:46:08.280 --> 00:46:11.310
to create 3D visualization
of the real world,

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:13.650
but also using simulations
to really mimic

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:17.250
and create the physical
simulation of the world.

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:19.500
And that's where it's
all coming together,

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:21.420
a unified special context

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:24.600
where you're bringing together
all the data into one place,

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:27.660
ability to drive the operations
not only on the digital world

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:30.510
but also on the physical world.

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:33.180
And the solution
here is in preview,

00:46:33.180 --> 00:46:36.420
where you can connect all this
data, all those streams of data

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:40.350
and embed it in the real-time
dashboard using OpenUSD scenes

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:43.170
to integrate it with NVIDIA
Omniverse libraries.

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:45.690
And this creates a 3D
visualization where you can see

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:48.300
in real time how those
end system operates,

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:49.350
but also the signals.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:52.770
And it's a bidirectional
cross-highlight capabilities.

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:56.100
And all of this is sparked by
a unified operational picture

00:46:56.100 --> 00:46:58.680
